{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"submission\": \"submission.json\", \"summary\": \"Submitted draft/submission.json (action=check) with draft/check-plan-window.json and draft/check-harness-window.go: the third scenario of the accepted requirement c-at-most-one-leader-per-term, aimed at the question its earlier review left open — whether the two-leader overlap of one term persists. The check reuses the overlap harness of the committed-entry check (a stale term write held inside a lagging voter's StableStore, an endpoint unreachable for one election, and a leadership transfer that makes that voter propose the term it already voted in), keeps the two leaders from reaching each other through the fabric's per-destination block, and then observes both nodes' published states and terms after a window of about eleven seconds; the asserted field is the second node's state, judged only while the first node still leads the admitted term and the second node's term equals that term, so applicability is exactly the requirement's case and the assertion is the requirement itself over the window rather than at the instant the overlap was created. I ran the harness twice before moving it into the draft: the test passes (exit 0) and reports leader_claim_observed with the sitting leader in Leader at term 3, then persistence_observed with the first leader still Leader at term 3 but the second node a Follower at the same term, with the third voter also a follower and a window of eleven seconds. The outcome is therefore a bounded negative rather than a violation, and the plan states that expectation explicitly and records the explanation it excludes: a higher term is impossible because the blocked peer cannot raise it and the reachable voter stayed in the same term, an unreachable peer is excluded because the block only covers the two leaders, and transport failures only back off replication without stepping a leader down — which leaves the lease check, since it counts a voter as contacted only within LeaderLeaseTimeout while a leader's contact refresh interval is derived from HeartbeatTimeout, so a lease shorter than that interval can end the leadership even though the peer is reachable. The submission also carries a handoff feedback that records the answered persistence question, the excluded explanations, and the new lead this raises as a separate question about the lease's coherence with the contact refresh interval for a ValidateConfig-accepted configuration, alongside the remaining production-TCP and snapshot-context items. Optional preflight with the trusted validator returned valid=true; the next step would be the correspondence review of this window scenario, which is a new artifact under the same accepted claim.\"}"}}
